# publish.log had no rotation — found 2026-08-22 at 1.1GB spanning six months
# (2026-02-22 onward) on the root fs, while the nginx tier logs rotate daily.
#
# The writers are systemd (`StandardOutput=append:`): publish.log from
# opencaselaw-publish.service, incremental_nightly.log from the Mon-Sat
# incremental service. systemd holds an O_APPEND fd for the life of a run, so
# `create` mode would rotate the name while the fd keeps writing to the old
# inode — copytruncate is required. With O_APPEND, writes after truncation
# land at the new EOF; no sparse-file artifact. The copy window can drop a few
# in-flight lines when rotation coincides with a running build; accepted for an
# operational log, and after the first (1.1GB) rotation the weekly copies are
# ~40MB and sub-second.
#
# maxsize is a guard for pathologically chatty runs: checked daily, so a run
# that logs gigabytes (e.g. a looping scraper) is cut at ~200MB rather than
# discovered at 3.6GB the way tier3 was on 2026-07-30.
#
# Apply:  cp deploy/logrotate/opencaselaw-publish /etc/logrotate.d/opencaselaw-publish
# Verify: logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/opencaselaw-publish
/opt/caselaw/repo/logs/publish.log /opt/caselaw/repo/logs/incremental_nightly.log {
    weekly
    maxsize 200M
    rotate 12
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    dateext
    copytruncate
}
